<node id="Amyloid Beta Accumulation and Plaque Formation">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Alzheimer’s disease is centrally characterized by the pathological accumulation of amyloid beta (Aβ) peptides, particularly Aβ42, in the brain. These peptides aggregate to form insoluble plaques, which disrupt neuronal communication and trigger a cascade of neurotoxic events, including oxidative stress, inflammation, and synaptic dysfunction. In ADNI data, this process is reflected by reduced CSF Aβ42 levels and increased amyloid tracer uptake on PET imaging.</data>
  <data key="d2">30</data>
</node>
