<node id="Neuropsychiatric and Behavioral Changes">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">AD is often accompanied by neuropsychiatric symptoms such as depression, agitation, irritability, apathy, hallucinations, and sleep disturbances. These behavioral changes, assessed by NPI-Q, GDS, and CDR, significantly impact quality of life and may precede or accompany cognitive impairment. Their presence often reflects the involvement of frontal and limbic structures in the disease.</data>
  <data key="d2">35</data>
</node>
<node id="Functional Decline in Daily Living">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Functional impairment in activities of daily living is a key clinical hallmark as AD progresses from MCI to dementia. This includes difficulties in memory, language, planning, organization, and attention. Both self-reported and informant-based Everyday Cognition (Ecog) scores, as well as the Functional Activities Questionnaire (FAQ), capture these real-world deficits and are crucial for staging disease severity.</data>
  <data key="d2">32</data>
</node>
<node id="Vascular and Cardiometabolic Risk Factors">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Vascular pathology, including hypertension and metabolic syndrome, increases risk for AD, possibly via reduced cerebral perfusion, microvascular injury, and facilitation of amyloid and tau pathology. Continuous variables such as blood pressure, weight, and height contribute to the assessment of vascular health and help distinguish vascular dementia comorbidity.</data>
  <data key="d2">32</data>
</node>
<node id="VSWEIGHT">
